# Test: can a female read a male's pattern?
# A test run by hand from the control page, to check the one thing you cannot see from
# outside - whether a female actually recognises the male in front of her. You choose the
# male and the female, press start, and watch her own lights, which the test borrows as a
# readout: head blue for male 1, head red for male 2, orange belly if she read "O",
# greenish belly if she read "P".
# Run it with the installation itself stopped - see the warning near the end.

0 You pick the sending male and the receiving female on the control page and press start. The page immediately shows the test as running, before anything has moved.
6 Three things move at once and the test waits for all three: the bar slides to bring that male to his meeting point with that female, and both bodies turn back to their own resting direction, so that the two end up facing each other. This takes a few seconds - longer if the bar has to cross to one of the far pairs.
0 If any of the three has not arrived within 30 seconds, the test carries on regardless and writes a warning in the log. So when a run looks wrong, read the log first: it will tell you whether the pair was ever actually in position.
0 Both of the male's appetites are forced to full, so he asks for both at once.
0 His ring lights up white and starts sending - ten flashes a fifth of a second each, two seconds in all, then dark for a little over two seconds before the next one. Expect the ring to be out about half the time: that is the message, not a fault.
0 The female starts reading the light in front of her.
0 From here neither body turns again for the whole run: they stay pointed at each other while he blinks. This is worth confirming by eye at the start of every run - stand where her head is pointing and check that the blinking ring really is in that line. If a resting direction is miscalibrated the pair will be facing slightly past each other, and the test will faithfully measure nothing.
2 For the first two seconds she cannot recognise anything - she needs one whole burst before she can even try.
1 From then on, once a second, a line is written to the results file and her readout lights are updated.
0 Her readout follows what she is seeing now: it lights up when she recognises someone, stays lit while she keeps seeing them, and goes dark again about nine seconds after she stops seeing anything. Nine seconds because he only sends every four and a third: anything shorter would blank the readout in every gap between his bursts. Watching it go dark is the readout working, not failing - block her view with your hand and it should go out within about nine seconds.
0 Every second is also written out in words, on the page and in the results file: "male1 wanting P - as expected", or "male2 wanting P - expected male1" when she got it wrong. The last minute of them is on the page newest-first, so a glitch can be watched rather than counted afterwards, and above it the wrong ones are tallied by what they said. That tally is the thing to read: one reading that keeps coming back is a pattern being mistaken for its neighbour every time, and a scatter of different ones is a poor view of him - which is the aiming, the distance or the room lighting, not the pattern list. It counts decodes and not seconds, and says both: "once, 3s in all" is one bad decode that stood for three seconds, because her readout holds an answer until something replaces it. Three seconds of the same wrong reading is one fault, not three.
0 One caution on a wrong reading: what she was expected to say is read at that moment, and the burst she decoded was sent up to four and a third seconds earlier. If his appetites cross the frustrated mark mid-run he really does change what he is asking for, and her correct reading of the older message is recorded as wrong for a few seconds. It looks like a run of the same mismatch. The expected-drive column in the file is what tells them apart - if it changes right there, the readings were right and the comparison was late.
0 The count on the page reads like "12 correct / 3 wrong / 25 nothing seen, out of 40 seconds" - one entry for each second of the run, so the three numbers always add up to the seconds elapsed. Expect the first two or three seconds to be "nothing seen": that is the blind window while she fills her memory. If a long run is mostly "nothing seen", she is not seeing him - check the aiming first, before suspecting the pattern.
0 A run always starts from nothing: no recognition, no lights, an empty memory. Nothing is carried over from an earlier run.
0 The results file is only complete once you press stop - it is written out at the end, so do not expect to watch it fill up line by line while the run is going.
0 The answer she gives is now correct whenever she reads the flashes cleanly - all six patterns a male can send are told apart properly. Two extra patterns used to be in her comparison list that no male ever sends, and one of them was a copy of another male's, which made "male 2 wants O" impossible for her to report at all. Those are gone.
0 She can still name the wrong male when her view is poor, though: one wrong flash out of ten is enough to tip the answer to a neighbouring pattern. So a wrong answer now means the light reaching her is bad - move on to the aiming, the distance, or the room lighting, not to the pattern list.
0 Warning: if the installation itself is running, this test fights it. The males start calling on their own, and the bar sets off wandering - sliding the male straight out of the alignment this test just made. Stop the installation before running this test.
0 You can change your mind mid-run: pick another male or another female on the control page and the test carries on rather than stopping. The current pair goes dark, the bar and both new bodies move into position - which takes a few seconds, and the page says so while it happens - and the new pair starts up. The count starts again from zero for the new pair, while the results file keeps every row, each one naming the pair it belongs to.
0 Pressing stop ends it: the male's ring goes out, her readout lights go out, the bar stays where it is. Stopping while the bodies are still moving stops it there and then, rather than waiting for them to arrive first.
